{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Query is now written in "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TypeScript"}]},{"type":"text","value":" to make sure the library and your projects are type-safe!"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Things to keep in mind:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Query follows "},{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/DefinitelyTyped/DefinitelyTyped#support-window","children":[{"type":"text","value":"DefinitelyTyped's support window"}]},{"type":"text","value":" and supports TypeScript versions released within the last 2 years. At the moment, that means TypeScript "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"5.6"}]},{"type":"text","value":" and newer."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Changes to types in this repository are considered "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"non-breaking"}]},{"type":"text","value":" and are usually released as "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"patch"}]},{"type":"text","value":" semver changes (otherwise every type enhancement would be a major version!)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"It is "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"highly recommended that you lock your angular-query-experimental package version to a specific patch release and upgrade with the expectation that types may be fixed or upgraded between any release"}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The non-type-related public API of TanStack Query and - after the experimental phase, the angular-query package still follow semver very strictly."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"type-inference","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Type Inference"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Types in TanStack Query generally flow through very well so that you don't have to provide type annotations for yourself"}]},{"type":"code","value":"@Component({\n  // ...\n  template: `@let data = query.data();`,\n  //               ^? data: number | undefined\n})\nclass MyComponent {\n  query = injectQuery(() => ({\n    queryKey: ['test'],\n    queryFn: () => Promise.resolve(5),\n  }))\n}","lang":"angular-ts"},{"type":"code","value":"@Component({\n  // ...\n  template: `@let data = query.data();`,\n  //               ^? data: string | undefined\n})\nclass MyComponent {\n  query = injectQuery(() => ({\n    queryKey: ['test'],\n    queryFn: () => Promise.resolve(5),\n    select: (data) => data.toString(),\n  }))\n}","lang":"angular-ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This works best if your "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryFn"},{"type":"text","value":" has a well-defined returned type. Keep in mind that most data fetching libraries return "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"any"},{"type":"text","value":" per default, so make sure to extract it to a properly typed function."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In this example we pass Group[] to the type parameter of HttpClient's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"get"},{"type":"text","value":" method."}]},{"type":"code","value":"@Component({\n  template: `@let data = query.data();`,\n  //               ^? data: Group[] | undefined\n})\nclass MyComponent {\n  http = inject(HttpClient)\n\n  query = injectQuery(() => ({\n    queryKey: ['groups'],\n    queryFn: () => lastValueFrom(this.http.get<Group[]>('/groups')),\n  }))\n}","lang":"angular-ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"type-narrowing","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Type Narrowing"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Query uses a "},{"type":"link","href":"https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/typescript-in-5-minutes-func.html#discriminated-unions","children":[{"type":"text","value":"discriminated union type"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for the query result, discriminated by the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"status"},{"type":"text","value":" field and the derived status boolean flags. This will allow you to check for e.g. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isSuccess()"},{"type":"text","value":" status to make "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":" defined:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"@Component({\n  // ...\n  template: `\n    @if (query.isSuccess()) {\n      @let data = query.data();\n      // ^? data: number\n    }\n  `,\n})\nclass MyComponent {\n  query = injectQuery(() => ({\n    queryKey: ['test'],\n    queryFn: () => Promise.resolve(5),\n  }))\n}","lang":"angular-ts"},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TypeScript currently does not support discriminated unions on object methods. Narrowing on signal fields on objects such as query results only works on signals returning a boolean. Prefer using "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isSuccess()"},{"type":"text","value":" and similar boolean status signals over "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"status() === 'success'"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"typing-the-error-field","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Typing the error field"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The type for error defaults to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Error"},{"type":"text","value":", because that is what most users expect."}]},{"type":"code","value":"@Component({\n  // ...\n  template: `@let error = query.error();`,\n  //                ^? error: Error | null\n})\nclass MyComponent {\n  query = injectQuery(() => ({\n    queryKey: ['groups'],\n    queryFn: fetchGroups,\n  }))\n}","lang":"angular-ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you want to throw a custom error, or something that isn't an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Error"},{"type":"text","value":" at all, you can specify the type of the error field:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"@Component({\n  // ...\n  template: `@let error = query.error();`,\n  //                ^? error: string | null\n})\nclass MyComponent {\n  query = injectQuery<Group[], string>(() => ({\n    queryKey: ['groups'],\n    queryFn: fetchGroups,\n  }))\n}","lang":"angular-ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"However, this has the drawback that type inference for all other generics of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"injectQuery"},{"type":"text","value":" will not work anymore. It is generally not considered a good practice to throw something that isn't an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Error"},{"type":"text","value":", so if you have a subclass like "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"AxiosError"},{"type":"text","value":" you can use "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"type narrowing"}]},{"type":"text","value":" to make the error field more specific:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import axios from 'axios'\n\nquery = injectQuery(() => ({ queryKey: ['groups'], queryFn: fetchGroups }))\n\ncomputed(() => {\n  const error = query.error()\n  //     ^? error: Error | null\n\n  if (axios.isAxiosError(error)) {\n    error\n    // ^? const error: AxiosError\n  }\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"registering-a-global-error","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Registering a global Error"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Query v5 allows for a way to set a global Error type for everything, without having to specify generics on call-sides, by amending the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Register"},{"type":"text","value":" interface. This will make sure inference still works, but the error field will be of the specified type. If you want to enforce that call-sides must do explicit type-narrowing, set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"defaultError"},{"type":"text","value":" to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"unknown"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import '@tanstack/angular-query-experimental'\n\ndeclare module '@tanstack/angular-query-experimental' {\n  interface Register {\n    // Use unknown so call sites must narrow explicitly.\n    defaultError: unknown\n  }\n}\n\nconst query = injectQuery(() => ({\n  queryKey: ['groups'],\n  queryFn: fetchGroups,\n}))\n\ncomputed(() => {\n  const error = query.error()\n  //      ^? error: unknown | null\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"typing-meta","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Typing meta"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"registering-global-meta","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Registering global Meta"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Similarly to registering a "},{"type":"link","href":"#registering-a-global-error","children":[{"type":"text","value":"global error type"}]},{"type":"text","value":" you can also register a global "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Meta"},{"type":"text","value":" type. This ensures the optional "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"meta"},{"type":"text","value":" field on "},{"type":"link","href":"./reference/functions/injectQuery.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"queries"}]},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"link","href":"./reference/functions/injectMutation.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"mutations"}]},{"type":"text","value":" stays consistent and is type-safe. Note that the registered type must extend "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Record<string, unknown>"},{"type":"text","value":" so that "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"meta"},{"type":"text","value":" remains an object."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import '@tanstack/angular-query-experimental'\n\ninterface MyMeta extends Record<string, unknown> {\n  // Your meta type definition.\n}\n\ndeclare module '@tanstack/angular-query-experimental' {\n  interface Register {\n    queryMeta: MyMeta\n    mutationMeta: MyMeta\n  }\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"typing-query-and-mutation-keys","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Typing query and mutation keys"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"registering-the-query-and-mutation-key-types","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Registering the query and mutation key types"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Also similarly to registering a "},{"type":"link","href":"#registering-a-global-error","children":[{"type":"text","value":"global error type"}]},{"type":"text","value":", you can also register a global "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"QueryKey"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"MutationKey"},{"type":"text","value":" type. This allows you to provide more structure to your keys, that matches your application's hierarchy, and have them be typed across all of the library's surface area. Note that the registered type must extend the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Array"},{"type":"text","value":" type, so that your keys remain an array."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import '@tanstack/angular-query-experimental'\n\ntype QueryKey = ['dashboard' | 'marketing', ...ReadonlyArray<unknown>]\n\ndeclare module '@tanstack/angular-query-experimental' {\n  interface Register {\n    queryKey: QueryKey\n    mutationKey: QueryKey\n  }\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"typing-query-options","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Typing Query Options"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you inline query options into "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"injectQuery"},{"type":"text","value":", you'll get automatic type inference. However, you might want to extract the query options into a separate function to share them between "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"injectQuery"},{"type":"text","value":" and e.g. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryClient.query"},{"type":"text","value":", or manage them in a service. In that case, you'd lose type inference. To get it back, you can use the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryOptions"},{"type":"text","value":" helper:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { noop } from '@tanstack/angular-query-experimental'\n\n@Injectable({\n  providedIn: 'root',\n})\nexport class QueriesService {\n  private http = inject(HttpClient)\n\n  post(postId: number) {\n    return queryOptions({\n      queryKey: ['post', postId],\n      queryFn: () => {\n        return lastValueFrom(\n          this.http.get<Post>(\n            `https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts/${postId}`,\n          ),\n        )\n      },\n    })\n  }\n}\n\n@Component({\n  // ...\n})\nexport class Component {\n  queryClient = inject(QueryClient)\n\n  postId = signal(1)\n\n  queries = inject(QueriesService)\n  optionsSignal = computed(() => this.queries.post(this.postId()))\n\n  postQuery = injectQuery(() => this.queries.post(1))\n  postQuery = injectQuery(() => this.queries.post(this.postId()))\n\n  // You can also pass a signal which returns query options\n  postQuery = injectQuery(this.optionsSignal)\n\n  someMethod() {\n    void this.queryClient.query(this.queries.post(23)).catch(noop)\n  }\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Further, the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryKey"},{"type":"text","value":" returned from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryOptions"},{"type":"text","value":" knows about the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryFn"},{"type":"text","value":" associated with it, and we can leverage that type information to make functions like "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryClient.getQueryData"},{"type":"text","value":" aware of those types as well:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"data = this.queryClient.getQueryData(groupOptions().queryKey)\n// ^? data: Post | undefined","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Without "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryOptions"},{"type":"text","value":", the type of data would be unknown, unless we'd pass a type parameter:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"data = queryClient.getQueryData<Post>(['post', 1])","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"typing-mutation-options","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Typing Mutation Options"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Similarly to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryOptions"},{"type":"text","value":", you can use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"mutationOptions"},{"type":"text","value":" to extract mutation options into a separate function:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"export class QueriesService {\n  private http = inject(HttpClient)\n\n  updatePost(id: number) {\n    return mutationOptions({\n      mutationFn: (post: Post) => Promise.resolve(post),\n      mutationKey: ['updatePost', id],\n      onSuccess: (newPost) => {\n        //           ^? newPost: Post\n        this.queryClient.setQueryData(['posts', id], newPost)\n      },\n    })\n  }\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"typesafe-disabling-of-queries-using-skiptoken","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Typesafe disabling of queries using "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"skipToken"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you are using TypeScript, you can use the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"skipToken"},{"type":"text","value":" to disable a query. This is useful when you want to disable a query based on a condition, but you still want to keep the query to be type safe.\nRead more about it in the "},{"type":"link","href":"./guides/disabling-queries.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Disabling Queries"}]},{"type":"text","value":" guide."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"type-inference","text":"Type Inference","level":2},{"id":"type-narrowing","text":"Type Narrowing","level":2},{"id":"typing-the-error-field","text":"Typing the error field","level":2},{"id":"registering-a-global-error","text":"Registering a global Error","level":3},{"id":"typing-meta","text":"Typing meta","level":2},{"id":"registering-global-meta","text":"Registering global Meta","level":3},{"id":"typing-query-and-mutation-keys","text":"Typing query and mutation keys","level":2},{"id":"registering-the-query-and-mutation-key-types","text":"Registering the query and mutation key types","level":3},{"id":"typing-query-options","text":"Typing Query Options","level":2},{"id":"typing-mutation-options","text":"Typing Mutation Options","level":2},{"id":"typesafe-disabling-of-queries-using-skiptoken","text":"Typesafe disabling of queries using skipToken","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nTanStack Query is now written in **TypeScript** to make sure the library and your projects are type-safe!\n\nThings to keep in mind:\n\n- TanStack Query follows [DefinitelyTyped's support window](https://github.com/DefinitelyTyped/DefinitelyTyped#support-window) and supports TypeScript versions released within the last 2 years. At the moment, that means TypeScript **5.6** and newer.\n- Changes to types in this repository are considered **non-breaking** and are usually released as **patch** semver changes (otherwise every type enhancement would be a major version!).\n- It is **highly recommended that you lock your angular-query-experimental package version to a specific patch release and upgrade with the expectation that types may be fixed or upgraded between any release**\n- The non-type-related public API of TanStack Query and - after the experimental phase, the angular-query package still follow semver very strictly.\n\n## Type Inference\n\nTypes in TanStack Query generally flow through very well so that you don't have to provide type annotations for yourself\n\n\n\n```angular-ts\n@Component({\n  // ...\n  template: `@let data = query.data();`,\n  //               ^? data: number | undefined\n})\nclass MyComponent {\n  query = injectQuery(() => ({\n    queryKey: ['test'],\n    queryFn: () => Promise.resolve(5),\n  }))\n}\n```\n\n\n\n\n```angular-ts\n@Component({\n  // ...\n  template: `@let data = query.data();`,\n  //               ^? data: string | undefined\n})\nclass MyComponent {\n  query = injectQuery(() => ({\n    queryKey: ['test'],\n    queryFn: () => Promise.resolve(5),\n    select: (data) => data.toString(),\n  }))\n}\n```\n\n\n\nThis works best if your `queryFn` has a well-defined returned type. Keep in mind that most data fetching libraries return `any` per default, so make sure to extract it to a properly typed function.\n\n\n\nIn this example we pass Group[] to the type parameter of HttpClient's `get` method.\n\n```angular-ts\n@Component({\n  template: `@let data = query.data();`,\n  //               ^? data: Group[] | undefined\n})\nclass MyComponent {\n  http = inject(HttpClient)\n\n  query = injectQuery(() => ({\n    queryKey: ['groups'],\n    queryFn: () => lastValueFrom(this.http.get<Group[]>('/groups')),\n  }))\n}\n```\n\n\n\n## Type Narrowing\n\nTanStack Query uses a [discriminated union type](https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/typescript-in-5-minutes-func.html#discriminated-unions) for the query result, discriminated by the `status` field and the derived status boolean flags. This will allow you to check for e.g. `isSuccess()` status to make `data` defined:\n\n\n\n```angular-ts\n@Component({\n  // ...\n  template: `\n    @if (query.isSuccess()) {\n      @let data = query.data();\n      // ^? data: number\n    }\n  `,\n})\nclass MyComponent {\n  query = injectQuery(() => ({\n    queryKey: ['test'],\n    queryFn: () => Promise.resolve(5),\n  }))\n}\n```\n\n> TypeScript currently does not support discriminated unions on object methods. Narrowing on signal fields on objects such as query results only works on signals returning a boolean. Prefer using `isSuccess()` and similar boolean status signals over `status() === 'success'`.\n\n\n\n## Typing the error field\n\nThe type for error defaults to `Error`, because that is what most users expect.\n\n\n\n```angular-ts\n@Component({\n  // ...\n  template: `@let error = query.error();`,\n  //                ^? error: Error | null\n})\nclass MyComponent {\n  query = injectQuery(() => ({\n    queryKey: ['groups'],\n    queryFn: fetchGroups,\n  }))\n}\n```\n\n\n\nIf you want to throw a custom error, or something that isn't an `Error` at all, you can specify the type of the error field:\n\n\n\n```angular-ts\n@Component({\n  // ...\n  template: `@let error = query.error();`,\n  //                ^? error: string | null\n})\nclass MyComponent {\n  query = injectQuery<Group[], string>(() => ({\n    queryKey: ['groups'],\n    queryFn: fetchGroups,\n  }))\n}\n```\n\n\n\nHowever, this has the drawback that type inference for all other generics of `injectQuery` will not work anymore. It is generally not considered a good practice to throw something that isn't an `Error`, so if you have a subclass like `AxiosError` you can use _type narrowing_ to make the error field more specific:\n\n\n\n```ts\nimport axios from 'axios'\n\nquery = injectQuery(() => ({ queryKey: ['groups'], queryFn: fetchGroups }))\n\ncomputed(() => {\n  const error = query.error()\n  //     ^? error: Error | null\n\n  if (axios.isAxiosError(error)) {\n    error\n    // ^? const error: AxiosError\n  }\n})\n```\n\n\n\n### Registering a global Error\n\nTanStack Query v5 allows for a way to set a global Error type for everything, without having to specify generics on call-sides, by amending the `Register` interface. This will make sure inference still works, but the error field will be of the specified type. If you want to enforce that call-sides must do explicit type-narrowing, set `defaultError` to `unknown`:\n\n\n\n```ts\nimport '@tanstack/angular-query-experimental'\n\ndeclare module '@tanstack/angular-query-experimental' {\n  interface Register {\n    // Use unknown so call sites must narrow explicitly.\n    defaultError: unknown\n  }\n}\n\nconst query = injectQuery(() => ({\n  queryKey: ['groups'],\n  queryFn: fetchGroups,\n}))\n\ncomputed(() => {\n  const error = query.error()\n  //      ^? error: unknown | null\n})\n```\n\n\n\n\n## Typing meta\n\n### Registering global Meta\n\nSimilarly to registering a [global error type](#registering-a-global-error) you can also register a global `Meta` type. This ensures the optional `meta` field on [queries](./reference/functions/injectQuery.md) and [mutations](./reference/functions/injectMutation.md) stays consistent and is type-safe. Note that the registered type must extend `Record<string, unknown>` so that `meta` remains an object.\n\n```ts\nimport '@tanstack/angular-query-experimental'\n\ninterface MyMeta extends Record<string, unknown> {\n  // Your meta type definition.\n}\n\ndeclare module '@tanstack/angular-query-experimental' {\n  interface Register {\n    queryMeta: MyMeta\n    mutationMeta: MyMeta\n  }\n}\n```\n\n\n\n\n## Typing query and mutation keys\n\n### Registering the query and mutation key types\n\nAlso similarly to registering a [global error type](#registering-a-global-error), you can also register a global `QueryKey` and `MutationKey` type. This allows you to provide more structure to your keys, that matches your application's hierarchy, and have them be typed across all of the library's surface area. Note that the registered type must extend the `Array` type, so that your keys remain an array.\n\n```ts\nimport '@tanstack/angular-query-experimental'\n\ntype QueryKey = ['dashboard' | 'marketing', ...ReadonlyArray<unknown>]\n\ndeclare module '@tanstack/angular-query-experimental' {\n  interface Register {\n    queryKey: QueryKey\n    mutationKey: QueryKey\n  }\n}\n```\n\n\n\n\n## Typing Query Options\n\nIf you inline query options into `injectQuery`, you'll get automatic type inference. However, you might want to extract the query options into a separate function to share them between `injectQuery` and e.g. `queryClient.query`, or manage them in a service. In that case, you'd lose type inference. To get it back, you can use the `queryOptions` helper:\n\n```ts\nimport { noop } from '@tanstack/angular-query-experimental'\n\n@Injectable({\n  providedIn: 'root',\n})\nexport class QueriesService {\n  private http = inject(HttpClient)\n\n  post(postId: number) {\n    return queryOptions({\n      queryKey: ['post', postId],\n      queryFn: () => {\n        return lastValueFrom(\n          this.http.get<Post>(\n            `https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts/${postId}`,\n          ),\n        )\n      },\n    })\n  }\n}\n\n@Component({\n  // ...\n})\nexport class Component {\n  queryClient = inject(QueryClient)\n\n  postId = signal(1)\n\n  queries = inject(QueriesService)\n  optionsSignal = computed(() => this.queries.post(this.postId()))\n\n  postQuery = injectQuery(() => this.queries.post(1))\n  postQuery = injectQuery(() => this.queries.post(this.postId()))\n\n  // You can also pass a signal which returns query options\n  postQuery = injectQuery(this.optionsSignal)\n\n  someMethod() {\n    void this.queryClient.query(this.queries.post(23)).catch(noop)\n  }\n}\n```\n\nFurther, the `queryKey` returned from `queryOptions` knows about the `queryFn` associated with it, and we can leverage that type information to make functions like `queryClient.getQueryData` aware of those types as well:\n\n```ts\ndata = this.queryClient.getQueryData(groupOptions().queryKey)\n// ^? data: Post | undefined\n```\n\nWithout `queryOptions`, the type of data would be unknown, unless we'd pass a type parameter:\n\n```ts\ndata = queryClient.getQueryData<Post>(['post', 1])\n```\n\n## Typing Mutation Options\n\nSimilarly to `queryOptions`, you can use `mutationOptions` to extract mutation options into a separate function:\n\n```ts\nexport class QueriesService {\n  private http = inject(HttpClient)\n\n  updatePost(id: number) {\n    return mutationOptions({\n      mutationFn: (post: Post) => Promise.resolve(post),\n      mutationKey: ['updatePost', id],\n      onSuccess: (newPost) => {\n        //           ^? newPost: Post\n        this.queryClient.setQueryData(['posts', id], newPost)\n      },\n    })\n  }\n}\n```\n\n\n\n## Typesafe disabling of queries using `skipToken`\n\nIf you are using TypeScript, you can use the `skipToken` to disable a query. This is useful when you want to disable a query based on a condition, but you still want to keep the query to be type safe.\nRead more about it in the [Disabling Queries](./guides/disabling-queries.md) guide.\n\n\n\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\nid: typescript\ntitle: TypeScript\nref: docs/framework/react/typescript.md\nreplace:\n  {\n    'useQuery': 'injectQuery',\n    'useMutation': 'injectMutation',\n    'react-query': 'angular-query-experimental',\n    'public API of React Query': 'public API of TanStack Query and - after the experimental phase, the angular-query package',\n    'still follows': 'still follow',\n    'React Query': 'TanStack Query',\n    '`success`': '`isSuccess()`',\n    'function:': 'function.',\n  }\n---\n\n[//]: # 'TypeInference1'\n\n```angular-ts\n@Component({\n  // ...\n  template: `@let data = query.data();`,\n  //               ^? data: number | undefined\n})\nclass MyComponent {\n  query = injectQuery(() => ({\n    queryKey: ['test'],\n    queryFn: () => Promise.resolve(5),\n  }))\n}\n```\n\n[//]: # 'TypeInference1'\n[//]: # 'TypeInference2'\n\n```angular-ts\n@Component({\n  // ...\n  template: `@let data = query.data();`,\n  //               ^? data: string | undefined\n})\nclass MyComponent {\n  query = injectQuery(() => ({\n    queryKey: ['test'],\n    queryFn: () => Promise.resolve(5),\n    select: (data) => data.toString(),\n  }))\n}\n```\n\n[//]: # 'TypeInference2'\n[//]: # 'TypeInference3'\n\nIn this example we pass Group[] to the type parameter of HttpClient's `get` method.\n\n```angular-ts\n@Component({\n  template: `@let data = query.data();`,\n  //               ^? data: Group[] | undefined\n})\nclass MyComponent {\n  http = inject(HttpClient)\n\n  query = injectQuery(() => ({\n    queryKey: ['groups'],\n    queryFn: () => lastValueFrom(this.http.get<Group[]>('/groups')),\n  }))\n}\n```\n\n[//]: # 'TypeInference3'\n[//]: # 'TypeNarrowing'\n\n```angular-ts\n@Component({\n  // ...\n  template: `\n    @if (query.isSuccess()) {\n      @let data = query.data();\n      // ^? data: number\n    }\n  `,\n})\nclass MyComponent {\n  query = injectQuery(() => ({\n    queryKey: ['test'],\n    queryFn: () => Promise.resolve(5),\n  }))\n}\n```\n\n> TypeScript currently does not support discriminated unions on object methods. Narrowing on signal fields on objects such as query results only works on signals returning a boolean. Prefer using `isSuccess()` and similar boolean status signals over `status() === 'success'`.\n\n[//]: # 'TypeNarrowing'\n[//]: # 'TypingError'\n\n```angular-ts\n@Component({\n  // ...\n  template: `@let error = query.error();`,\n  //                ^? error: Error | null\n})\nclass MyComponent {\n  query = injectQuery(() => ({\n    queryKey: ['groups'],\n    queryFn: fetchGroups,\n  }))\n}\n```\n\n[//]: # 'TypingError'\n[//]: # 'TypingError2'\n\n```angular-ts\n@Component({\n  // ...\n  template: `@let error = query.error();`,\n  //                ^? error: string | null\n})\nclass MyComponent {\n  query = injectQuery<Group[], string>(() => ({\n    queryKey: ['groups'],\n    queryFn: fetchGroups,\n  }))\n}\n```\n\n[//]: # 'TypingError2'\n[//]: # 'TypingError3'\n\n```ts\nimport axios from 'axios'\n\nquery = injectQuery(() => ({ queryKey: ['groups'], queryFn: fetchGroups }))\n\ncomputed(() => {\n  const error = query.error()\n  //     ^? error: Error | null\n\n  if (axios.isAxiosError(error)) {\n    error\n    // ^? const error: AxiosError\n  }\n})\n```\n\n[//]: # 'TypingError3'\n[//]: # 'RegisterErrorType'\n\n```ts\nimport '@tanstack/angular-query-experimental'\n\ndeclare module '@tanstack/angular-query-experimental' {\n  interface Register {\n    // Use unknown so call sites must narrow explicitly.\n    defaultError: unknown\n  }\n}\n\nconst query = injectQuery(() => ({\n  queryKey: ['groups'],\n  queryFn: fetchGroups,\n}))\n\ncomputed(() => {\n  const error = query.error()\n  //      ^? error: unknown | null\n})\n```\n\n[//]: # 'RegisterErrorType'\n[//]: # 'TypingQueryOptions'\n\n## Typing Query Options\n\nIf you inline query options into `injectQuery`, you'll get automatic type inference. However, you might want to extract the query options into a separate function to share them between `injectQuery` and e.g. `queryClient.query`, or manage them in a service. In that case, you'd lose type inference. To get it back, you can use the `queryOptions` helper:\n\n```ts\nimport { noop } from '@tanstack/angular-query-experimental'\n\n@Injectable({\n  providedIn: 'root',\n})\nexport class QueriesService {\n  private http = inject(HttpClient)\n\n  post(postId: number) {\n    return queryOptions({\n      queryKey: ['post', postId],\n      queryFn: () => {\n        return lastValueFrom(\n          this.http.get<Post>(\n            `https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts/${postId}`,\n          ),\n        )\n      },\n    })\n  }\n}\n\n@Component({\n  // ...\n})\nexport class Component {\n  queryClient = inject(QueryClient)\n\n  postId = signal(1)\n\n  queries = inject(QueriesService)\n  optionsSignal = computed(() => this.queries.post(this.postId()))\n\n  postQuery = injectQuery(() => this.queries.post(1))\n  postQuery = injectQuery(() => this.queries.post(this.postId()))\n\n  // You can also pass a signal which returns query options\n  postQuery = injectQuery(this.optionsSignal)\n\n  someMethod() {\n    void this.queryClient.query(this.queries.post(23)).catch(noop)\n  }\n}\n```\n\nFurther, the `queryKey` returned from `queryOptions` knows about the `queryFn` associated with it, and we can leverage that type information to make functions like `queryClient.getQueryData` aware of those types as well:\n\n```ts\ndata = this.queryClient.getQueryData(groupOptions().queryKey)\n// ^? data: Post | undefined\n```\n\nWithout `queryOptions`, the type of data would be unknown, unless we'd pass a type parameter:\n\n```ts\ndata = queryClient.getQueryData<Post>(['post', 1])\n```\n\n## Typing Mutation Options\n\nSimilarly to `queryOptions`, you can use `mutationOptions` to extract mutation options into a separate function:\n\n```ts\nexport class QueriesService {\n  private http = inject(HttpClient)\n\n  updatePost(id: number) {\n    return mutationOptions({\n      mutationFn: (post: Post) => Promise.resolve(post),\n      mutationKey: ['updatePost', id],\n      onSuccess: (newPost) => {\n        //           ^? newPost: Post\n        this.queryClient.setQueryData(['posts', id], newPost)\n      },\n    })\n  }\n}\n```\n\n[//]: # 'TypingQueryOptions'\n[//]: # 'Materials'\n[//]: # 'Materials'\n"}
